+# ConflictSet — the Resolver's history, selected at startup
+
+Test source: `fdbserver/resolver/ConflictSet.cpp`, the cases under `/fdbserver/conflictset/` and
+the benchmark `noSim/fdbserver/conflictset/benchmark`. Upstream's own cases in the same file
+(`/fdbserver/skiplist/...`) are not described here. This report covers the first integration of the
+paged temporal conflict set into the Resolver, and the first comparison of its cost with FDB's skip
+list. The section [The paged B+ as a third history](#the-paged-b-as-a-third-history-2026-09-29)
+adds the B+ prototype, integrated on 2026-09-29; everything before it describes the paged skip
+list as of 2026-09-28.
+
+What ran: the working tree on branch `paged-resolver` on 2026-09-28, last commit `6239f4d43a`, with
+uncommitted changes to the files listed below; upstream base `ef6d39c877`. Binary: `bin/fdbserver`
+of the Release build tree `$ROOT/beyond-5s-build`, built in the image
+`foundationdb/build:rockylinux9-latest` with clang 19.1.5, `-O3 -DNDEBUG`.
+
+## What changed
+
+| file | change |
+|---|---|
+| `fdbserver/core/include/fdbserver/core/Knobs.h`, `fdbserver/core/ServerKnobs.cpp` | `RESOLVER_USE_PAGED_CONFLICT_SET` (bool, default false) and `PAGED_RESOLVER_POOL_PAGES` (int64, pages of 64 KiB, default 16384) |
+| `fdbserver/resolver/ConflictSet.cpp`, `ConflictSet.h` | the history is chosen when the set is built; three batch operations branch on it; `getConflictSetStats`; the integration cases and the benchmark |
+| `fdbserver/resolver/Resolver.cpp` | batch-phase times and, for the paged set, pool, boundary and rejection counters in `ResolverMetrics` |
+| `fdbserver/resolver/PagedTemporalSkipList.h` | `TemporalConflictSet::reclaimedHistoryPages()`, read-only, for the metric that shows whether pool pressure reclaimed history |
+| `fdbserver/resolver/CMakeLists.txt` | the two standalone batteries are no longer compiled into `fdbserver_resolver` |
+
+### How the selection works
+
+`newConflictSet()` reads the two knobs when the Resolver is constructed, after knob overrides have
+been applied, and builds exactly one history: `std::optional<SkipList>` or
+`std::unique_ptr<TemporalConflictSet>`. The choice holds for the set's lifetime. The paged set
+starts with a boundary of zero.
+
+Everything in the batch other than the history is the same code in both modes: collecting and
+sorting the ranges, the too-old window (`req.version - MAX_WRITE_TRANSACTION_LIFE_VERSIONS`,
+decided in `addTransaction` and only for transactions with read ranges), the intra-batch check with
+`MiniConflictSet`, and the combination of accepted writes. Three operations branch once per batch:
+
+- **Reading history.** The skip list's interleaved `detectConflicts`, or, for the paged set:
+  - **Admission.** A transaction whose read version is below the paged boundary is marked too old,
+    so it neither commits nor contributes writes. The boundary is read once, since nothing moves it
+    while reads are checked, and all of a transaction's ranges share its read version.
+  - **Ranges.** `TemporalConflictSet::hasRangeConflict` for each read, against the range index.
+  - **Points.** Every read still undecided goes to `hasPointConflicts` in one batch: sixteen
+    interleaved, prefetching searches of the point index.
+  - **Reporting.** Every range of a transaction that reports conflicting keys is queried, and each
+    conflicting one is recorded once, as the skip list does. Without reporting, a transaction's
+    remaining ranges are skipped once it conflicts.
+  - **Errors.** No query throws on this path; `hasConflict`, which does throw `transaction_too_old`,
+    remains for single queries.
+- **Recording writes.** `addConflictRanges` on the skip list, or one call to
+  `TemporalConflictSet::addWrites` with every combined range at the batch version. A range is a
+  point when `equalsKeyAfter(begin, end)` holds (the predicate behind
+  `KeyRangeRef::singleKeyRange()`). `addWrites` inserts in groups of sixteen: prepare, check the
+  boundary, find the paths interleaved, link. It drops the rest of a batch once reclamation
+  reaches its version; see
+  [resolver-performance.md](../../design/beyond-5s/resolver-performance.md#6-grouped-insertion-2026-09-28).
+- **After the batch.** The skip list's bounded `removeBefore(newOldestVersion)`, or the paged set's
+  `cleanup()` with its knob-driven budget. Urgent reclamation stays where it was, inside the pool.
+
+### The two boundaries
+
+The window decides `too_old` exactly as before, in both modes. The paged set adds its own boundary,
+`validationMinRV`, which starts at zero and moves only when pool pressure reclaims a history page.
+It moves before the page is reused, never back, and never below what the set still holds.
+
+A read the window admits but the paged boundary rejects is a transaction whose needed history the
+pool has already discarded. It becomes `TransactionTooOld` and is counted as
+`PagedTooOldWithinWindow`. `PagedBoundaryPastWindow` records the largest distance by which the
+boundary has passed the window. With a pool large enough for the window, both stay at zero.
+
+Consequence: the paged set keeps history until capacity requires otherwise, not only for the
+window. With a large pool its memory is the pool it is given, not the window's worth of writes; see
+the measurements.
+
+## Correctness
+
+### Integration cases
+
+Each case except the last builds batches as `CommitTransactionRef`s and resolves them through
+`ConflictBatch::addTransaction` and `detectConflicts`, as the Resolver does. It runs once with each
+history and expects the same outcomes. The window is 1000 versions.
+
+| case | property | skip list | paged | paged B+ |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| `historyFromAnEarlierBatch` | a range and a point written in one batch conflict with a later batch's reads below their version, not at it; reads touching either end of the range, just after the point or ending at it do not | OK | OK | OK |
+| `conflictWithinTheBatch` | a read of a key an earlier transaction in the batch writes conflicts; a read beginning where that write ends does not; a committed transaction's write reaches a later reader in the same batch | OK | OK | OK |
+| `aTransactionDoesNotConflictWithItself` | reading and writing the same ranges in one transaction commits | OK | OK | OK |
+| `rejectedWritesAreNotRecorded` | the writes of a transaction that conflicts, and of one too old, reach neither a later reader in the same batch nor the next batch | OK | OK | OK |
+| `tooOldAndTransactionsWithoutReads` | one version below the window is too old, at it is admitted; a transaction without reads is never too old, and its write is recorded | OK | OK | OK |
+| `reportedConflictingRanges` | every range conflicting with history is reported (1 and 3 of four); a conflict found only inside the batch reports its first range; a transaction that does not ask reports nothing | OK | OK | OK |
+| `pagedBoundaryRejectsAsTooOld` | paged only: a three-page pool reclaims history; a read inside the window but below the boundary is too old and counted, one at the boundary is admitted, a transaction without reads commits | — | OK | OK |
+
+The B+ column is from 2026-09-29: each case now runs once per history (`forEachHistory`), and
+`pagedBoundaryRejectsAsTooOld` once per paged history.
+
+Command, from any directory:
+
+```bash
+docker run --rm -v $ROOT:$ROOT -w "$PWD" foundationdb/build:rockylinux9-latest \
+  $ROOT/beyond-5s-build/bin/fdbserver -r unittests -f /fdbserver/conflictset/
+```
+
+Each case's verdict is the `Severity` of its `UnitTest` trace event (10 passed, 40 failed). All
+seven were 10, as was the one upstream case under `/fdbserver/skiplist/`.
+
+### Mutations
+
+Eleven mutations of the paged branch in `ConflictSet.cpp`. Each was compiled from a scratch copy,
+swapped into a copy of `libfdbserver_resolver.a` with `llvm-ar`, and linked into an `fdbserver` of
+its own; the build tree was not touched. The harness is
+`$ROOT/scratch/integration/mut/` (`mutate.py`, `run-one.sh`).
+
+| mutation | detected by |
+|---|---|
+| a read rejected by the paged boundary becomes a conflict | `pagedBoundaryRejectsAsTooOld` |
+| it becomes a commit | `pagedBoundaryRejectsAsTooOld` |
+| a reporting transaction stops at its first history conflict | `reportedConflictingRanges` |
+| conflicting ranges are not reported | `reportedConflictingRanges` |
+| a history conflict is ignored | 4 cases, including `historyFromAnEarlierBatch` |
+| every write is recorded as a point | `historyFromAnEarlierBatch`, `reportedConflictingRanges` |
+| range writes are recorded one version early | `historyFromAnEarlierBatch` |
+| no write is recorded | 5 cases, including `rejectedWritesAreNotRecorded` |
+| the paged set is never built | `pagedBoundaryRejectsAsTooOld` (the other cases then run the skip list twice) |
+| every write is recorded as a range | not detected; equivalent: `[k, keyAfter(k))` as a range conflicts exactly when the point does |
+| no batch cleanup | not detected; equivalent in outcomes: cleanup only recycles cells whose writes have expired |
+
+Nine are caught by named cases, two are equivalent, and none was caught only by an assertion or a
+crash.
+
+These mutations ran against the first reading path, which caught `transaction_too_old` from
+`hasConflict` for every read. The path now checks admission against the boundary once, queries the
+range index per read and batches the point reads; see
+[resolver-performance.md](../../design/beyond-5s/resolver-performance.md#5-interleaved-point-searches-2026-09-28).
+The seven integration cases pass on it, but the mutations have not been rerun.
