+## First performance comparison
+
+### Method
+
+`noSim/fdbserver/conflictset/benchmark` drives the batch path itself: `ConflictBatch`,
+`addTransaction` and `detectConflicts`, the code the Resolver runs for each batch. It does not
+involve the network, the proxies, logging or any other role. It measures the index as the batch
+uses it, not a cluster.
+
+- **One binary for both indexes.** The two differ only in `--knob_resolver_use_paged_conflict_set`
+  and, for the paged set, `--knob_paged_resolver_pool_pages`.
+- **Isolation.** Each run is a new process pinned to one core, run one at a time with nothing else
+  running on the machine (16 cores, 62 GiB). The figures below ran with `taskset -c 3`. From
+  2026-09-30 every timed run uses core 7 with CPU 15 idle, in a container started with
+  `--cpuset-cpus=7 --cpuset-mems=0` (performance record, sections 24 and 25).
+- **Timers.** Batch generation is outside every timed interval. The timers are per batch and per
+  phase, never per node or per range.
+- **Build.** Release (`-O3`), no sanitizer, no simulation.
+
+Configuration, the same in every run:
+
+| parameter | value |
+|---|---|
+| batches | 1000 of warm-up, then 2000 measured |
+| per batch | 500 transactions, 4 read ranges and 2 write ranges each |
+| keys | 10-byte keys, uniform over 10^7 |
+| points | reads and writes of one key, `[k, keyAfter(k))` |
+| ranges | reads of 1 to 1000 keys, writes of 1 to 10 |
+| versions | 10^4 per batch; read versions up to 2·10^6 behind; window `MAX_WRITE_TRANSACTION_LIFE_VERSIONS` = 5·10^6, so 500 batches |
+| seeds | 1, 2, 3 for each configuration |
+
+Command for one run (runner: `$ROOT/scratch/integration/bench/bench.sh`):
+
+```bash
+docker run --rm -v $ROOT:$ROOT -w "$PWD" foundationdb/build:rockylinux9-latest \
+  taskset -c 3 $ROOT/beyond-5s-build/bin/fdbserver -r unittests \
+  -f noSim/fdbserver/conflictset/benchmark --test_workload=points --test_seed=1 \
+  --test_warmupBatches=1000 --test_batches=2000 --test_transactionsPerBatch=500 \
+  --test_readsPerTransaction=4 --test_writesPerTransaction=2 --test_keySpace=10000000 \
+  --test_versionsPerBatch=10000 --test_maxReadLagVersions=2000000 --test_maxReadKeys=1000 \
+  --test_maxWriteKeys=10 \
+  --knob_resolver_use_paged_conflict_set=1 --knob_paged_resolver_pool_pages=4096
+```
+
+What each column means:
+
+- **Phase times** are measured-period totals, in seconds, of the Resolver's own phase counters:
+  reading history, the intra-batch check, combining writes, recording writes (which includes any
+  urgent reclamation, since that runs inside the pool) and end-of-batch cleanup.
+- **RSS** is the process's resident memory after the measured period. **Pool** is the paged set's
+  pages in use.
+
+In every run both indexes committed exactly the same transactions for the same seed: 962526,
+962551 and 962199 of 1,000,000 for points, 164926, 164850 and 164856 for ranges. No transaction
+was too old, by the window or by the paged boundary.
+
+### Results
+
+Means of three seeds; the range across seeds is in brackets where it exceeds 2%.
+
+**Points**
+
+| index | pool | tx/s | batch p50 | p99 | read | record | cleanup | RSS | pool used | reclaimed |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
+| skip list | — | 237,659 | 2.11 ms | 2.25 ms | 2.11 s | 1.29 s | 0.40 s | 183 MiB | — | — |
+| paged | 4096 | 74,220 | 6.73 ms | 7.08 ms | 8.71 s | 4.29 s | 0.06 s | 310 MiB | 256 MiB | 958 pages |
+| paged | 16384 | 62,673 | 8.07 ms | 8.87 ms | 10.41 s | 5.13 s | 0.00 s | 850 MiB | 795 MiB | 0 |
+
+**Ranges**
+
+| index | pool | tx/s | batch p50 | p99 | read | record | cleanup | RSS | pool used | reclaimed |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
+| skip list | — | 487,774 | 1.03 ms | 1.08 ms | 1.49 s | 0.16 s | 0.02 s | 76 MiB | — | — |
+| paged | 4096 | 8,138 [7,940–8,446] | 61.9 ms [59.5–63.3] | 90.7 ms [87.9–93.3] | 121.8 s [117.2–124.8] | 0.73 s | 0.00 s | 196 MiB | 142 MiB | 0 |
+| paged | 16384 | 8,114 [7,858–8,432] | 62.2 ms [59.5–64.3] | 91.6 ms | 122.2 s [117.4–126.1] | 0.73 s | 0.00 s | 196 MiB | 142 MiB | 0 |
+
+Intra-batch check and combination cost the same in both indexes: 0.014 s and 0.019 s for points,
+0.009–0.024 s and 0.017–0.021 s for ranges.
+
+**Pool against the window.** With 4096 pages the points workload reclaims history continuously, 958
+pages, while the boundary never passes the window: `PagedBoundaryPastWindow` is 0. The pool holds
+about twice the window. With 16384 pages it reclaims nothing and holds all 3000 batches. In the
+ranges workload fewer transactions commit, and neither pool fills: the paged set keeps all 3000
+batches, six times the window the skip list keeps. Its memory is what it has been given to fill,
+not the window's worth.
+
+Two further single runs, seed 1, outside the table:
+
+- **Ranges, 640 pages.** Reclamation keeps retention near the window: 220 pages reclaimed, 720,490
+  cells recycled, boundary never past the window. Throughput falls to **549 tx/s**, with a batch
+  p50 of **917 ms**. Reading history takes 1819 s of the 1820 s measured; recording writes 0.43 s.
+- **Points, 1400 pages.** 112,472 tx/s, batch p50 4.44 ms. This is not a valid configuration: the
+  boundary passed the window by 1,750,000 versions. No read was rejected only because this
+  workload's reads lag at most 2·10^6 versions.
+
+### What the figures say, and what they do not
+
+- **The paged set is slower in every configuration measured here.** For points, reading history
+  costs 4.1× the skip list's and recording writes 3.3×; batch p50 is 3.2× higher. For ranges,
+  reading history costs 82× without reclamation and about 1200× with it.
+- **The cost is in reading history, and grows with what the range search cannot rule out.** The
+  phase times localize it; the reasons below are hypotheses from the code, not measured:
+  - Summaries never become tighter: without repair, a split copies the whole predecessor span's
+    bounds to both halves. So older spans keep a `maxEnd` from writes that are no longer in them,
+    and a range read must descend into spans to its left that hold nothing it could meet.
+  - Once reclamation expires sources and cleanup transfers summaries, `NeedsRepair` spreads and
+    `maxEnd` stops pruning altogether. That fits the fifteen-fold drop between 4096 and 640 pages.
+  - Confirming it needs a count of the spans each query explores, by summary state: a counter, not
+    a timer.
+- **For points the gap is smaller, with no pruning problem.** The point search does not use
+  summaries. Plausible causes, not measured:
+  - one independent descent per read, where the skip list interleaves sixteen with prefetching;
+  - reaching every key through the heap's page table;
+  - the cost of building each tower's cells and summaries on insertion.
+- **Retention.** The paged set's memory is its pool, and a larger pool makes it slower: 16384 pages
+  against 4096 for points. Its history is not bounded by the window.
+- **Scope.** These figures are one core running the batch path with synthetic uniform keys. They
+  say nothing about a cluster's end-to-end latency, about skewed or adversarial keys, or about
+  memory beyond RSS and pool pages.
+
