+## Long reads over a longer history
+
+`noSim/fdbserver/conflictset/longReads` runs `skipListTest`'s workload with every read at a
+version drawn uniformly from the admitted window. It runs on one resolver or on several, each
+owning an equal part of the key space: transactions are clipped per resolver, as a commit proxy
+clips them, and commit only if every resolver accepts them.
+
+Parameters: `index`, `resolvers`, `poolPages` (per resolver), `windowVersions`, `oldReads`,
+`warmupBatches`, `batches`, `transactionsPerBatch`, `seed`.
+
+It prints:
+- work (summed over resolvers) and wall (maximum per batch) time;
+- phase times, batch p50/p99, decisions and their hash;
+- each paged resolver's pool, boundary, margin over the window and rejections.
+
+Results: resolver-performance.md, section 13. Runner and analysis:
+`$ROOT/scratch/longreads/run.sh`, `analyze.py`.
+
+## What this does not cover
+
+- **Empty read ranges.** The paged set never reports a conflict for `[k, k)`. Reading the skip
+  list's `CheckMax` suggests it can, when the segment holding `k` is newer than the read; that was
+  not run. No case produces an empty read range, so the two modes were not compared on one.
+- **`clearConflictSet`** has no caller. It asserts the skip list is selected, since the paged set
+  cannot start over as if every key had been written at one version.
+- **Errors other than `transaction_too_old`** from the paged set propagate unchanged, but no case
+  injects one. `std::bad_alloc` from an undersized pool ends the Resolver, as settled; no case
+  exercises it through the Resolver.
+- **Recovery, multiple resolvers and key-range splits** run in the simulations only as the tests
+  above exercise them.
+- **The long-transaction protocol.** The window is the ordinary one; nothing here extends it.
