+# PagedTemporalSkipListTests — test report
+
+Battery: `fdbserver/resolver/PagedTemporalSkipListTests.cpp`. Components under test: `SkipEntry`,
+`SkipLevel`, `CleanupList`, `HeapPageTable`, `TemporalHeap`, `IndexPageAllocator` and
+`TemporalSkipList`, the reclamation policy of `TemporalConflictSet` together with the `PagePool`
+hook it installs, and conflict detection (`hasConflict`, `hasPointConflict`, `hasRangeConflict`),
+declared in `PagedTemporalSkipList.h` and implemented in
+`PagedTemporalSkipList.cpp`. `PagePool` has its own battery and its own report.
+
+Last run: 2026-09-28, on snapshots of the working tree on branch `paged-resolver`, whose last
+commit was `6239f4d43a`, with `PagedTemporalSkipList.h` and `.cpp` modified and not committed;
+upstream base `ef6d39c877`.
+
+- **The reclamation cases and their mutations** ran on the snapshot of 2026-09-27 at 23:42:32.
+- **The budget cases and their mutations** ran on the snapshot of 2026-09-28 at 03:16:58.
+- **The conflict cases, their mutations, and the final run of the whole battery** ran on the
+  snapshot of 2026-09-28 at 05:56:27, with one line added to its header.
+
+The working tree was then brought up to date and the battery was run on it directly:
+
+- **The header** declares `hasRangeConflictRec` as a private member.
+- **`SkipEntry::endKeyOf`** is defined, and the range search reads a record's begin and end
+  through `beginWriteKey` and `endKeyOf` instead of through `getRange` directly.
+- **`aRangeCellReadsItsEnd`** was added for it.
+
+The five interleaved-search cases and their mutations ran on the working tree snapshotted on
+2026-09-28 at 20:39:31. The seven grouped-insertion cases, their mutations and the final run of the
+whole battery ran on the tree snapshotted at 22:49:26, in which a batch's writes are inserted in
+groups. In both, the point search is a resumable, prefetching state machine, and the search counters
+are built in only when `PAGED_RESOLVER_SEARCH_STATS` is 1. `run-paged-tests.sh` sets it, since some
+cases count summary transfers.
+
+Earlier sections keep the snapshot they were measured on. The tree of 06:26:38 had already passed
+107 of 107; the conflict mutations and their coverage below come from it.
+
+That last snapshot did not compile as it stood. `PagedTemporalSkipList.cpp` defines
+`TemporalSkipList::hasRangeConflictRec`, which the header did not declare. The copy the battery
+ran against adds, as a private member of `TemporalSkipList`, the declaration matching that
+definition, and nothing else. The reclamation policy and `cleanup()` are the same in all three
+snapshots. The report ships in the same commit as the code it
+describes, so it names the base rather than its own commit.
+
+This revision replaces the fixed-capacity blocks with individual cells. `TemporalSkipBlock`,
+`KeyPermutation`, `EntryRef` and logical slot positions are gone, and with them the fifteen
+cases that tested them. The results of the block version — its 40 cases, its mutations and its
+coverage — belong to that version and are not carried over. Every figure below was measured on
+this one.
+
+## What the components are
+
+**`SkipEntry`** is one cell of the index: an individual, immovable object naming one heap
+record, with its kind, its level and the record's commit version stored outside the heap. It
+carries its own key-order links (`previous`, `next`), its links to the copies of the same
+record at adjacent levels (`down`, `up`), its place in the cleanup queue (`newerForCleanup`,
+the one temporal link) and the summary of the span it covers (`forward`). `beginWriteKey` reads the point
+key, or the interval's begin, from the heap. `linkedInLevel` says whether the cell is linked
+into a level. The default constructor yields a detached, free cell with every field initialized;
+page ID zero is never registered, so it names no live record.
+
+**`SkipLevel`** is one level of an index: its two ends, `first_` and `last_`, public fields; the
+summary of the span before its first cell; and two operations on the horizontal links.
+`insertAfter` asserts that the cell is not linked and the predecessor is, links the cell after
+that predecessor, or at the head for a null one, overwriting the cell's own links without reading
+them, and sets `linkedInLevel`. `unlink` asserts the cell is linked, removes it, clears the mark
+and leaves the cell's own links as they were; it no longer asserts that its neighbours point back
+at it. Neither compares keys, touches vertical or cleanup links, or maintains summaries.
+
+**`TemporalSkipList`** is one index: 26 levels, its kind, and the heap its keys live in.
+`findInsertionPath` walks from the highest level to zero and records the upper-bound predecessor
+at each; a cell at or below `validationMinRV` is not compared but taken out with its tower by
+`unlinkExpiredTower`, whose summaries `transferSummary` folds into the predecessor or the prefix.
+`insert` draws a height with `randomLevel` (`ctz(~bits)` over a copy of `ConflictSet.cpp`'s
+generator), acquires one cell per level, searches, and for each level has `splitSummary` give the
+new cell its summary, links it after the saved predecessor, queues it for cleanup and connects it
+to the copy below; above the tower it widens the existing spans' summaries. `repairMaxEnd` is
+written and called by nothing.
+
+**`IndexPageAllocator`** packs cells into pool pages: a small header, then as many cells as fit.
+A new page's cells are all constructed and linked into one doubly linked free list; `pageOf`
+finds a cell's page by masking its address. `acquire` pops and reinitializes a cell, taking a page
+first if none is free and throwing `bad_alloc` if the pool has none. `release` pushes a cell back
+without returning its page; `releaseEmptyPages` returns the pages with no live cell, taking their
+cells out of the free list first. The destructor returns every page.
+
+`PagePool::acquire`, which the heap and the allocator now call, is tested by the pool's battery;
+its `gc()` has an empty body. `TemporalConflictSet`'s operations are declared without bodies and
+nothing here calls them.
+
+**`HeapPageTable`** resolves a logical `PageID` to its current physical page. Pages are
+appended and reclaimed oldest first, so the live identifiers form a contiguous range of at most
+`capacity` identifiers, and a fixed-size ring is exact under any modulus of at least `capacity`.
+Since heapPageMask (`2ad8a1151`) the ring has the capacity rounded up to a power of two and is
+indexed by `id & mask`, with no division; the capacity as given still limits the live pages. It is
+allocated once, at construction, which is what keeps the index of pages inside the page budget.
+
+**`TemporalHeap`** appends point and range conflict records into 64 KiB pages taken from the
+pool, hands out `HeapRef`s into them, and reclaims the oldest page on demand, reporting the
+newest commit version that page held.
+
+## Tools
+
+| | |
+|---|---|
+| Image | `foundationdb/build:rockylinux9-latest` |
+| Compiler | clang 19.1.5, `-std=c++20 -stdlib=libc++ -Wall -Wextra` |
+| Linked against | the fork's `libfdbserver_core`, `libfdbclient`, `libfdbrpc` and `libflow`: the conflict set reads its cleanup knobs from `SERVER_KNOBS` |
+| Sanitizers | the same compile line with `-O1 -g -fno-omit-frame-pointer -fsanitize=address,undefined -fno-sanitize-recover=undefined`, run with `ASAN_OPTIONS=detect_leaks=0`; the FDB libraries it links are not instrumented |
+| Build and run | `./run-paged-tests.sh [build-dir] [battery]`, which reads the include flags from the real build's `build.ninja` so they cannot drift from it |
+| Coverage | the same compile line with `-O0 -fprofile-instr-generate -fcoverage-mapping -fcoverage-mcdc`, then `llvm-profdata merge -sparse`, `llvm-cov report --show-functions`, `llvm-cov show --show-branches=count --show-mcdc` and `llvm-cov export`, all LLVM 19.1.5 |
+
+Standalone binary: no network, no simulator, no trace file. A failing `ASSERT` terminates the
+process instead of reporting a case. Standard output is unbuffered and each case prints its
+name before it runs, so a run that aborts still shows every verdict before it and the case in
+which the assertion fired. An abort in a case's teardown — the heap's destructor, at the end of
+the case's scope — appears after that case's verdict. Components run bottom up — cells, levels,
+the queue, the heap, the allocator, the skip list — so a fault in a lower layer is met first by
+its own cases rather than by the index cases built on it.
+
+## Methodology
+
+- **One property per case.** The verdict is printed on the case's own line; failure messages
+  follow it, indented.
+- **No unguarded `Optional::get`.** Every read goes through a presence gate (`appended`,
+  `pointAt`, `rangeAt`, `versionOf`) that records a failure and returns a harmless value, so a
+  regression is reported as a failed case and the rest of the battery still runs.
+- **Differential testing against a model** for `SkipLevel`: a `std::vector` of cell pointers,
+  checked after every edit by walking the level forward from `first()` and back from `last()`,
+  with both ends and `empty()` compared too, and every cell in the model marked as linked. Each
+  cell is tagged in its `recordCV`, so a case can tell that linking a cell rewrote nothing but its
+  horizontal links and its mark.
+- **The same for `CleanupList`**, against a `std::deque`: after every step `oldestCV()` must be
+  the model head's version and the temporal links, walked from that head, must visit exactly
+  the model in order and end on a null link. The queue's ends are private, so the walk starts
+  from the model's head; `oldestCV()` and `popOldest()` are what tie it to the queue's own.
+  Calls that require a non-empty queue are gated on `empty()`, so a mutation that loses a cell
+  is reported as a failed case instead of tripping the queue's own assertion.
+- **The index is read back through its own search.** Levels are private, so a case recovers
+  each level by searching for a key larger than any written: the predecessor at each level is
+  its last cell, and walking back from it visits the whole level. Against a model of the writes
+  it checks the level-zero order — by key, equal keys in insertion order — and that every tower
+  is sound: each level ordered, each cell above zero sitting on the copy of its record below,
+  each level a subsequence of the one below. Summaries are checked against what their spans
+  hold: `maxCV` at least every live version, a usable `maxEnd` readable and at least every live
+  end, `Empty` never, and exact at level zero while nothing has been removed.
+- **A cell needs a real record.** `SkipEntry` stores a reference, not key bytes, so every cell
+  case builds the record in a heap first and reads the key back through it.
+- **Byte-exact reads.** Keys are arbitrary bytes: empty keys, embedded zero bytes and keys of
+  nothing but zero bytes are read back and compared byte for byte, so no length is ever
+  recomputed from the contents.
+- **Private state, for the reclamation cases only.** This file includes the header with
+  `private` defined as `public`, after including everything the header itself includes, so only
+  the header's own declarations change. The reclamation cases read the queue, the free list, the
+  levels and the heap's newest page, set `validationMinRV_` and knob-derived fields directly,
+  and call `reclaimForAllocation` directly where an insertion would read a reclaimed key before
+  the case could look. Production code gains no accessor and no `friend`.
+- **Deterministic heights.** The promotion generator is a `thread_local` that starts at zero in
+  every thread. Each reclamation case that depends on heights runs on a new thread and mirrors
+  the generator (`LevelMirror`), consuming values through `randomLevel()` until the next
+  insertion gets the height it needs. Nothing is restored because nothing outlives the thread.
+  An exception escaping such a case is caught and recorded as a failure of that case.
+- **Knobs** a case changes are set through `setServerKnob` before the set is constructed, since
+  the set captures them there, and restored on scope exit.
+- **Accounting.** Pages: free, heap and index add up to the pool's limit. Cells: every cell of
+  every index page is free, queued, or one the case took for itself; the cells per page are
+  learned from the allocator, since the page layout is not visible here.
+- **Mutation checks** in a scratch copy of the implementation; the battery must fail.
+- **Coverage measured and classified**, with every uncovered arm accounted for.
+
