+### Reclamation
+
+The policy under test: a batch `cleanup()` has a budget and always finishes the tower it started;
+an exhausted pool runs reclamation synchronously and retries once; that urgent reclamation may
+exceed the batch budget; the boundary advances before history is reclaimed; and a pool too small
+may fail, with no minimum enforced and no recovery of a partial insertion.
+
+| case | property |
+|---|---|
+| `availablePagesDoNotInvokeReclamation` | with free pages, `PagePool::acquire` does not call the reclaimer and moves the counters as `tryAcquire` does; a conflict set whose writes fit leaves the boundary where it was, reclaims no history, and counts every inserted cell |
+| `exhaustedPoolReclaimsAndRetries` | on an exhausted pool, the same `acquire` call returns the page a test reclaimer frees, after exactly one call that was told the use requested, and the page moves from heap to index accounting; a reclaimer that frees nothing leaves the call failing after one more try |
+| `indexAllocationReusesCellsWithoutAFreePage` | no free page and no free cell, but expired towers: the insertion succeeds with no page changing hands, no history reclaimed and the boundary unmoved, because expired towers, oldest first, were recycled into cells |
+| `heapAllocationReusesAnEmptyIndexPage` | a full pool of one heap page and two index pages whose cells have all expired: a record that needs a new heap page gets an index page emptied by cleanup, and no history is discarded |
+| `historyReclamationAdvancesValidationBoundary` | driven directly, reclaiming the oldest heap page leaves the boundary exactly at that page's newest version when it returns; then, over 300 writes to a three-page pool, every reclamation leaves the boundary covering the reclaimed page's newest version, it never goes back, references into reclaimed pages no longer resolve, and every retained record reads back with its version |
+| `insertionUsesBoundaryAdvancedDuringCellAllocation` | the new record fits on the newest heap page, so history is reclaimed while the insertion acquires its cells, after the record is written and before the search: the insertion completes, the new write is in the index with its key, and every live cell's record is readable and in key order |
+| `urgentReclamationExceedsBatchBudget` | with a batch budget of one cell, a batch pass cleans one tower; the heap allocation that follows cleans every expired cell and succeeds without discarding history |
+| `urgentReclamationPreservesInsertionAccounting` | points and ranges both add the cells they insert to the counter; urgent reclamation during an insertion leaves it holding everything inserted since the last batch pass, and only the batch pass resets it |
+| `batchCleanupFinishesTheStartedTower` | a first tower steered to at least three cells, a one-cell budget: the pass returns the tower's height, every cell of it is free, and the next tower is still the head and still linked |
+| `cleanupAcceptsPreviouslyDetachedTowers` | a tower detached by a search for its own key is recycled by the next pass, and the links, `maxCV` and level prefix summaries of every kept cell are unchanged; and a range tower detached while its level-zero predecessor was live, whose predecessor's cell is then recycled and reused by a new write before the tower leaves the queue: recycling the tower merges nothing into the new write's summary |
+| `cleanupStopsAtALiveHead` | an empty queue yields no work; a live head yields none; a live head with an expired tower behind it — built by inserting a lower version after a higher one, as the queue's approximate order allows — yields none and the expired tower stays, until the head expires and both go |
+| `undersizedPoolFailsWithoutRecyclingTheCurrentRecord` | a two-page pool with no expired tower and no free cell: reclamation for an index page, driven directly, leaves the only heap page alone; the insertion then throws `std::bad_alloc` without reclaiming that page or moving the boundary, earlier records on it stay readable, and the set is destroyed without further use |
