+### Conflict detection
+
+A read is `[begin, end)`. A write conflicts with it when its version is above the read version
+and it overlaps the read: a point when `begin <= key < end`, a range when
+`writeBegin < readEnd && writeEnd > readBegin`. A read version below the boundary is rejected with
+`transaction_too_old`. The cases steer every height, so the structure each one names is the one it
+builds, and each checks that structure before relying on it.
+
+| case | property |
+|---|---|
+| `readsBelowTheBoundaryAreTooOld` | a read version one below the boundary is rejected, by an empty set, where nothing could conflict and where something would; one equal to it is admitted; a point or range written at the read version does not conflict, and one written a version later does |
+| `pointConflictsRespectHalfOpenReads` | a point at the read's begin conflicts and one at its end does not; points before and after it do not, including when the search runs to the end of the list; empty and inverted reads conflict with nothing; of two writes to one key the newer decides; an old node followed by a conflicting one conflicts, and not when that one sits at the read's end |
+| `rangeOverlapIsHalfOpen` | equal ranges, a write inside the read, a read inside the write, and an overlap on either side conflict; separate ranges and ranges that touch at either end do not; empty and inverted reads do not; a write at the read version does not. Then a tower whose span holds a newer write, so that its summary cannot decide: at level 1 its own record is compared under the same rules, at the read version and at either touching end |
+| `prefixesAndEmptyLevelsAreSearched` | a conflict before the first tower of levels 1 and 2, in their prefixes, is found and a touching read is not; with every level above zero empty, a conflict at level zero is found; a conflict that exists only at level zero, inside a tall tower's span, is found |
+| `recursionCoversEverySpanInTurn` | three towers on level 1: the first span is explored and holds no conflict, the second can be discarded by version, the third holds the conflict and is the last of its level; the search returns from the first and finds it in the third; without it the answer is no. A write with the same begin as the second tower, inserted before it, is found in the first span, just before the stop; one inserted after it, in the second span; an old one at the stop is not a conflict |
+| `conservativeSummariesAloneDoNotConflict` | a span whose `maxCV` comes from one write and whose `maxEnd` from another is explored but yields no conflict, while the long write does conflict below its own version; a span of old writes is discarded by version, and one whose greatest end is the read's begin is discarded by end |
+| `unusableSummariesAreExploredNotTrusted` | a tower's span and a level's prefix marked `NeedsRepair` are explored, their conflict found and nothing invented; a `Valid` summary whose source has expired, page still live, is explored and neither the expired write nor the stale bound conflicts; a `Valid` summary whose source's page has been reclaimed is explored, finds the live range, and reads nothing from that page |
+| `expiredNodesStillDelimitLiveSpans` | a tall tower expires and its record's page is reclaimed, the boundary covering it first; live writes in its span are found, including a later one after an earlier one; reads past them and above every version find nothing; the range search changes no link, no mark and no queue membership, and the expired tower stays linked |
+| `theOwnerConsultsBothIndexes` | a conflict only in points, only in ranges, in both and in neither, and each index answers only for its own writes |
+| `answersSurviveRepairMarksSearchesAndCleanup` | 400 writes and 300 queries from seed 71, 174 of them conflicts, compared with the model: at first, repeated, after marking every prefix and a third of the range summaries `NeedsRepair`, after point searches that unlink expired towers, and after `cleanup()`; the structures stay sound |
+| `conflictsAgreeWithAModelUnderReclamation` | for seeds 1 to 4, 6000 operations each on an eight-page pool: writes, batch passes and queries, compared with the model; see below |
+
+**The model.** It keeps every write the case made, as its kind, its keys and its version, and
+answers a query by walking that list with the two formulas above. It uses no summary, no search
+path and no helper from the code under test. Writes the conflict set has reclaimed stay in the
+model; they are at or below the boundary, so they cannot conflict with an admitted read.
+
+**The generated run.** Each seed starts from a new thread, so heights are reproducible too.
+Keys are a random prefix of up to three letters padded to up to 700 bytes, so heap pages fill
+and history is reclaimed. Ranges and reads are mostly narrow, a key and that key with one more
+letter, and a quarter wide. Read versions fall within 200 of the newest write. Queries go to the
+point index, the range index or the owner, each against the matching model. About one query in
+ten asks below the boundary and must be rejected; the rest are admitted. On any disagreement the
+case records the seed, the operation number, the query, the expected and actual answers, and the
+last twelve operations. Measured:
+
+| seed | admitted queries | conflicts | rejected as too old | history pages reclaimed |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| 1 | 2682 | 1939 | 279 | 17 |
+| 2 | 2745 | 2012 | 279 | 17 |
+| 3 | 2701 | 1910 | 267 | 18 |
+| 4 | 2715 | 1959 | 283 | 17 |
+
+No disagreement occurred.
