+### Interleaved point searches
+
+`hasPointConflicts` advances up to sixteen point searches one step at a time, and a step may
+unlink expired towers that another search is waiting on. Each case runs searches through
+`startPointSearch` and `stepPointSearch` directly: it stops one where the case chooses, runs another
+to its end, then resumes the first.
+
+Summary transfers are counted exactly (`summaryTransfers()`): each cell leaving its level must
+transfer once, however many searches find its tower expired.
+
+| case | property |
+|---|---|
+| `aSearchPausedOnAnExpiredCellResumesAfterAnotherUnlinksIt` | a search waiting to read an expired cell at level zero resumes after another search has unlinked its tower, follows the cell's `next`, and finds the conflict after it; one transfer in all, and the index is sound |
+| `aSearchFollowsSeveralCellsUnlinkedWhileItWaited` | three consecutive expired cells are unlinked while a search waits on the first; it follows the stale `next` links through all three to the live cell and its conflict; three transfers in all |
+| `searchesWaitingAtDifferentLevelsSurviveUnlinking` | one search waits on an expired tower's top cell at level 2 while descending, another on its bottom cell while scanning; a third unlinks the tower; both resume and find their conflicts; three transfers in all |
+| `aSearchWaitingOnARecordSeesCellsUnlinkedAfterIt` | a search that has read a live cell and waits on its record resumes after two expired cells following it were unlinked, and goes straight on to the next live cell |
+| `batchedPointSearchesAgreeWithAModel` | batches of 1, 15, 16, 17 and 400 queries against 1500 point writes, half of them expired, agree with a model that walks the writes; for 16 or more, both answers occur and towers are unlinked; the index is sound |
+
+### Grouped insertion
+
+`TemporalConflictSet::addWrites` prepares a group of up to sixteen writes (records and cells, where
+reclamation may run), checks the boundary, finds their paths with interleaved searches, and links
+them with `linkGroup`: towers in decreasing key order, then the growth above each tower in
+increasing order, into the span that owns it.
+
+The structural cases drive `prepare`, `findInsertionPaths` and `linkGroup` directly, with steered
+heights; the others go through `addWrites`.
+
+| case | property |
+|---|---|
+| `preparedWritesSharingPredecessorsLinkInOrder` | four writes between two tall towers, found against the index before the group and all having the same predecessor, are linked in key order at levels 0, 1 and 2; every cell is linked, the index is sound and accounted, and each new write is found |
