+
+Measured separately, on the snapshot named at the top, from this battery's own profile, with the
+same toolchain. Only the functions the policy added or changed are listed; the figures above
+belong to the earlier revision and were not remeasured.
+
+| function | lines | branch arms not taken |
+|---|---|---|
+| `PagePool::acquire`, `PagePool::gc` | all | none |
+| `TemporalHeap::oldestPageNewestCV` | all | none |
+| `TemporalSkipList::detach` | all | none |
+| `TemporalConflictSet::cleanupExpired` | all | none; MC/DC 100% on its loop condition |
+| `TemporalConflictSet::reclaimForAllocation` | all but 2 | `!newestCV.present()`; MC/DC 100% on the guard on the last heap page |
+| `IndexPageAllocator::acquirePage` | all but 2 | storage obtained *and* cells recycled |
+| `TemporalConflictSet::addPoint`, `addRange` | all but 2 each | the heap append failing |
+| `TemporalConflictSet::cleanup` (second snapshot, with the budget cases) | all | 1 of 14: `limitPages > 0` false, and with it that condition's MC/DC pair; the boost and lag decisions are at 100% MC/DC |
+| the constructor | all | 20 arms, the failure arms of its six knob `ASSERT`s |
+
+| not taken | class | why |
+|---|---|---|
+| `acquirePage`: storage and recycled cells together | excluded by the policy | an index request returns as soon as cleanup recycles a tower, and cleanup returns no page, so the retry finds no page; and when it reclaims history instead, it recycled no cell |
+| `addPoint`/`addRange`: the heap append failing; `reclaimForAllocation`: no heap page to reclaim | reachable only when index cells outlive the history they name | a heap request can fail only if the pool holds no heap page and every index cell is live; live cells name records on heap pages, and cells whose records were reclaimed are expired and recyclable. It takes cells outside the queue: a test that holds them, or those an insertion that failed leaves behind, after which the set must be destroyed. Not built here |
+| `cleanup`: `limitPages > 0` false | excluded by invariant | a pool always has at least one page |
+| the constructor's knob checks | assertion failure arms | the knob validation was checked by a separate probe program, not by this battery |
+
+### Coverage of conflict detection
+
+Measured on the working tree as snapshotted on 2026-09-28 at 06:26:38, from this battery's own
+profile.
+
+| function | lines | branch arms not taken |
+|---|---|---|
+| `TemporalConflictSet::hasConflict` | all | none |
+| `TemporalSkipList::hasPointConflict` | all | none |
+| `TemporalSkipList::hasRangeConflict` | all | none |
+| `TemporalSkipList::hasRangeConflictRec` | all but 1 | 1 of 46: a summary with a `maxCV` above the read version and an `Empty` end |
+| `SkipEntry::endKeyOf` | all | 3 of 4, all failure arms of its `ASSERT` on the kind and of `Optional::get` |
+
+| not taken | class | why |
+|---|---|---|
+| `hasRangeConflictRec`: `explore = false` for an `Empty` end | excluded by invariant | a range summary gains a `maxCV` only together with an end: `splitSummary` gives every new cell its own end, growth above a tower copies the new write's end into an `Empty` one, and a transfer into an `Empty` target copies the source's. So any span with a version has an end that is `Valid` or `NeedsRepair`. The arm is a guard that the current maintenance cannot reach |
+
+## Current limitations
+
+**`beginWriteKey` requires the cell's record to still have its page.** It asserts that the record is
+readable. The search reads keys only of cells newer than the boundary. So this holds as long as
+`reclaimForAllocation` advances the boundary before it reclaims, and `insert` reads the boundary
+after acquiring its cells; both are true in the code. A cell of an expired write that no search
+has met still names its record, and reading its key would abort.
+
+- **Advance, then reclaim.** No concurrency, callback or yield separates the two steps, so their
+  order cannot be told apart from outside, as the equivalent mutation shows. The settled order is
+  kept and reviewed; no test can hold it.
+- **The boundary by reference.** `insert` takes it by `const Version&` so that it sees an advance
+  made while its cells are acquired. Passing it by value would compile. Its regression case,
+  `insertionUsesBoundaryAdvancedDuringCellAllocation`, still fails: the search reads a reclaimed
+  key and the assertion escapes the call, which the case records as a failure. That is detection
+  by an unexpected throw rather than by a check of the result, and no more machinery is added for
+  it.
+
+**A failed insertion leaves its cells behind.** When an allocation fails, `addPoint` and
+`addRange` throw `std::bad_alloc` after the record is already in the heap. Any cells already
+acquired for the tower are neither queued nor free. As settled, nothing recovers them and the set
+must be destroyed; `undersizedPoolFailsWithoutRecyclingTheCurrentRecord` checks only that it can
+be.
+
+**Urgent reclamation for the heap is unbounded.** It cleans every expired cell before deciding
+whether it has to discard history. That is the settled policy. The latency of one such insertion
+grows with the backlog of expired cells, and remains to be measured under load with accumulated
+garbage; correctness cases cannot say what it costs.
+
+**`reclaimOldest`'s `>` comparison is redundant under the heap's contract**, as the equivalent
+mutation shows. It protects only against a caller that appends a lower commit version after a
+higher one, which the contract forbids and nothing asserts.
+
+**`TemporalHeap::releasePage` is declared and has no definition.** Nothing calls it, so it does
+not fail to link, but it is dangling.
+
+## What this battery does not cover
+
+- **`repairMaxEnd`**, written and called by nothing.
+- **Promotion heights on purpose, outside the reclamation cases.** Those cases steer heights by
+  running on a new thread and mirroring the generator. The older cases do not, so shapes such as
+  a tower of full height or an expired promoted cell first on its level occur only as chance
+  allows; see the coverage.
+- **What is not written**: the repair of `NeedsRepair` summaries, which is `repairMaxEnd`'s
+  callers, and pruning point searches by `maxCV`. Both are optimizations to be judged by
+  performance; these cases measure none, and say nothing about how much a search costs.
+- **The cost of searching.** The equivalent mutations show that exploring more is invisible to
+  these cases; so would be a search that explores far more than it needs to.
+- **Level prefix summaries, directly.** They are still not checked against the spans they
+  cover. The conflict cases cover them only through their effect: a prefix that failed to bound
+  its span would lose a conflict there, as the prefix cases and the model run would show.
+- **Two reclamations nested inside one insertion**, and a failed allocation on the heap side; see
+  the reclamation coverage.
+- **The order of advancing the boundary and reclaiming.** It cannot be observed in one thread;
+  it is kept by review. It would matter to another thread only if concurrent access to one set
+  were allowed, and the design provides no synchronization for that.
+- **The cost of urgent reclamation**, which needs a load measurement, not a correctness case.
+- **The delay the queue's order can cause.** With versions out of order, an expired cell can
+  wait behind a live head; nothing here measures how long.
+- **The preconditions of `SkipLevel`, `CleanupList` and `IndexPageAllocator`**: inserting a cell
+  already linked, a predecessor from another level, appending a cell already queued, popping an
+  empty queue, releasing a cell twice or one of another allocator. Some trip an assertion; none
+  is promised to.
+- **Violations of the caller's contract**: a record read with the wrong kind, a record larger
+  than a page, a range with `begin >= end`, decreasing commit versions, reclaiming a heap page
+  before the boundary covers it. Some assertions catch some of these; none is promised.
+- **Sequences longer than those written.** The random cases run a few hundred to twenty
+  thousand steps from fixed seeds; nothing enumerates operation sequences.
+- **MC/DC is not multiple-condition coverage.** It requires each condition to determine the
+  outcome on its own, not every combination of conditions.
+- **Concurrency.** None of these structures is thread-safe, and none is exercised from more
+  than one thread.
diff --git a/fdbserver/resolver/PairIndex.h b/fdbserver/resolver/PairIndex.h
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..f1f03d45ef6eb466df82594fc7e3d97762d07b1f
--- /dev/null
+++ b/fdbserver/resolver/PairIndex.h
@@ -0,0 +1,47 @@
+#pragma once
+#include <array>
+#include <cstdint>
+#include <limits>
+#include <stdexcept>
+namespace by5v5 {
+constexpr std::uint32_t absent=std::numeric_limits<std::uint32_t>::max();
+struct PairLink {
+    std::uint32_t previous=absent,next=absent;
+    std::uint8_t priority=0;
+};
+template<unsigned C=23> class PairIndex {
+    static_assert(C<=31 && C>0);
+    std::array<std::uint32_t,2*C+2> heads;
+    std::uint64_t mask=0;
+public:
+    PairIndex() { heads.fill(absent); }
+    std::uint32_t head(unsigned priority) const { return heads.at(priority); }
+    std::uint32_t candidate() const {
+        if (!mask) return absent;
+        const unsigned priority = std::numeric_limits<unsigned long long>::digits - 1u -
+                                  __builtin_clzll(static_cast<unsigned long long>(mask));
+        return heads[priority];
+    }
+    template<class Lookup> void set(std::uint32_t leftId,unsigned priority,Lookup&& get) {
+        if(leftId==absent || priority>2*C+1) throw std::invalid_argument("pair index");
+        auto& x=get(leftId);unsigned old=x.priority;
+        if(old>2*C+1) throw std::logic_error("priority");
+        if(old==priority) return;
+        if(old) {
+            if(x.previous!=absent) get(x.previous).next=x.next;
+            else {
+                if(heads[old]!=leftId) throw std::logic_error("membership");
+                heads[old]=x.next;
+            }
+            if(x.next!=absent)get(x.next).previous=x.previous;
+            if(heads[old]==absent)mask&=~(std::uint64_t(1)<<old);
+        }
+        x.priority=static_cast<std::uint8_t>(priority);x.previous=x.next=absent;
+        if(priority) {
+            x.next=heads[priority];
+            if(x.next!=absent)get(x.next).previous=leftId;
+            heads[priority]=leftId;mask|=std::uint64_t(1)<<priority;
+        }
+    }
+};
+}
diff --git a/fdbserver/resolver/Redistribution.h b/fdbserver/resolver/Redistribution.h
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..11c886a0b39ec1709ae804873d52d6b9e5c50c8c
--- /dev/null
+++ b/fdbserver/resolver/Redistribution.h
@@ -0,0 +1,58 @@
+#pragma once
+#include <cstdint>
+#include <stdexcept>
+namespace by5v5 {
+enum class InsertAction { Here, ConsumeHere, MoveLeft, MoveRight, Split };
+struct Neighbor { bool exists=false; unsigned real=0,fictitious=0; bool structuralSafe=false; };
+struct InsertPlan {
+    InsertAction action=InsertAction::Split;
+    unsigned consume=0;
+    bool incomingGoesToNeighbor=false;
+    unsigned incomingRank=0;
+};
+template<unsigned C=23> unsigned effective(unsigned real,unsigned fictitious) {
+    if(real>C || fictitious>C-real) throw std::invalid_argument("occupancy");
+    return real+fictitious;
+}
+template<unsigned C=23>
+InsertPlan planInsert(unsigned real,unsigned fictitious,unsigned rank,const Neighbor& left,const Neighbor& right) {
+    auto e=effective<C>(real,fictitious);
+    if(rank>real) throw std::invalid_argument("rank");
+    if(e<C) return {InsertAction::Here,0,false,rank};
+    if(fictitious) return {InsertAction::ConsumeHere,1,false,rank};
+    for(unsigned side=0;side<2;++side) {
+        const auto& n=side?right:left;
+        if(!n.exists) continue;
+        auto ne=effective<C>(n.real,n.fictitious);
+        if(!n.structuralSafe || (ne==C && !n.fictitious)) continue;
+        unsigned consume=ne==C?1:0;
+        if(!side) {
+            bool incoming=rank==0;
+            return {InsertAction::MoveLeft,consume,incoming,incoming?n.real:rank-1};
+        }
+        bool incoming=rank==real;
+        return {InsertAction::MoveRight,consume,incoming,incoming?0:rank};
+    }
+    return {InsertAction::Split,0,false,rank};
+}
+struct FusionPlan { bool possible=false; unsigned left=0,right=0; };
+template<unsigned C=23>
+FusionPlan planFusion(unsigned lr,unsigned lf,unsigned rr,unsigned rf,bool sameParent,unsigned maxMerged=17,unsigned maxSource=6) {
+    effective<C>(lr,lf);effective<C>(rr,rf);
+    if(!maxMerged || maxMerged>C || maxSource>C) throw std::invalid_argument("fusion limits");
+    if(!sameParent || !lr || !rr || lr+rr>maxMerged || (lr>maxSource && rr>maxSource)) return {};
+    unsigned total=lr+lf+rr+rf,need=total>maxMerged?total-maxMerged:0,best=2*C+1;
+    FusionPlan result;
+    for(unsigned side=0;side<2;++side) {
+        if((side?rr:lr)>maxSource) continue;
+        unsigned a=0,b=0;
+        if(!side) a=lr+lf>maxSource?lr+lf-maxSource:0;
+        else b=rr+rf>maxSource?rr+rf-maxSource:0;
+        unsigned remaining=need>a+b?need-a-b:0;
+        auto da=remaining<lf-a?remaining:lf-a;a+=da;remaining-=da;
+        auto db=remaining<rf-b?remaining:rf-b;b+=db;remaining-=db;
+        if(!remaining && a+b<best) { result={true,a,b};best=a+b; }
+    }
+    return result;
+}
+}
