@@ -0,0 +1,149 @@
+# Paged B+ conflict set: configuration (v105)
+
+The paged B+ is selected with `RESOLVER_USE_PAGED_CONFLICT_SET=true` and `PAGED_RESOLVER_USE_BTREE=true`. From v104
+its read path, leaf fusion and grouped preparation are the only implementation: there are no switches to compile them
+out or disable them at run time. Earlier behaviour is reproduced from earlier commits, not from options in this one.
+
+## Reader
+
+One reader: the two-pass block reader. Endpoints are sorted once per batch (or taken already ordered from the batch
+path), and the tree is read level by level in blocks of nodes sized to the L2 budget.
+
+- **Upper levels.** Internal nodes at depths `1..breadthBoundaryLevels` (root depth 1) locate each separator among the
+  node's sorted endpoints by binary search. `breadthBoundaryLevels` is 2; zero selects grouped sweeps at every level.
+- **Lower levels and leaves.** Grouped preparation: each endpoint compares inline prefixes boundary by boundary, in
+  groups of G boundaries. A tied prefix marks a heap candidate; candidates are resolved and prefetched, and the next
+  round completes them with whole-key comparisons. A frame-wide common prefix proves the bytes every endpoint shares.
+- **Configurations.** Exactly two:
+
+  | configuration | G | boundary mask |
+  |---|---|---|
+  | default | 3 | copied to a local before the boundary loop |
+  | alternative | 2 | read from the node state at each boundary |
+
+  The mask choice follows G and is resolved once per prepared group, outside the loop over boundaries. Both use the
+  group countdown and advance to the next boundary with the frame's local end.
+
+| setting | where | default | values |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `PAGED_BTREE_READER_GROUP_SIZE` | server knob | 3 | 2 or 3; anything else fails an assertion when the set is built |
+| `ReadPipelineConfig::sweepGroupSize` | component API | 3 | 2 or 3; `std::invalid_argument` otherwise |
+| `ReadPipelineConfig::breadthBoundaryLevels` | component API | 2 | 0 to `MaxHeight` |
+| `ReadPipelineConfig::l2Bytes`, `l2ReserveBytes` | component API | 1 MiB, 64 KiB | block budget is half of their difference |
+| `ReadPipelineConfig::lineBytes` | component API | 64 | power of two up to 4096 |
+
+`PagedBTreeConflictSet::configureReader(G)` sets the G used by `hasConflicts` and `hasConflictsOrdered`;
+`readerConfig()` returns the configuration they use.
+
+## Leaf fusion
+
+After an overwrite shrinks a leaf, the leaf may merge with one neighbour under the same parent, chosen at random. The
+attempt probability depends on the leaf's occupancy (78, 73, 68, 64, 59 and 52 % for one to six entries), drawn from
+FDB's `deterministicRandom()`. Internal nodes never merge.
+
+| parameter | where | default |
+|---|---|---|
+| `PAGED_BTREE_PROB_FUSION_MAX_SOURCE` | macro, also `ProbFusionConfig::maxSourceEntries` | 6; 1 to capacity |
+| `PAGED_BTREE_PROB_FUSION_MAX_RESULT` | macro, also `ProbFusionConfig::maxMergedEntries` | 3/4 of capacity (17 at C23); 2 to capacity |
+
+The probabilities were measured at capacity 23 with result limit 17; at other capacities or limits they are the same
+table, no longer a measurement.
+
+## Fictitious leaf occupancy and pressure fusion
+
+From v105 every leaf counts real entries and fictitious units, and is critical when their sum reaches the capacity C.
+The reserve is always active; there is no switch to disable it.
+
+- **Creation.** The first leaf of an empty tree starts with `rootReserve` units. Each leaf split adds `splitReserve`
+  units, divided between the two halves so that the larger half is as small as possible; equal plans are chosen at
+  random from a generator seeded with `choiceSeed`. Replenishment is permanent: splits keep adding units after real
+  history starts being discarded.
+- **Insertion into a critical leaf.** A leaf holding fictitious units releases one instead of splitting. Otherwise it
+  first moves entries to an adjacent leaf with room, and splits only when neither neighbour can take them.
+- **Pressure fusion.** When an insertion needs a node and there is no free node, no retired node and no free index
+  page, the tree merges two adjacent leaves under the same parent whose real entries fit `pressureMergedLimit`,
+  removing fictitious units as needed. Candidate pairs are indexed, and the pair with the most reclaimable capacity
+  (2C minus the real entries of both leaves) is taken first. Only when no pair qualifies does the allocator advance the
+  floor and reclaim by age.
+
+| parameter | where | default | values |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `FictitiousLeafConfig::rootReserve` | component API | C − 3 (20 at C23) | below C |
+| `FictitiousLeafConfig::splitReserve` | component API | C − 3 (20 at C23) | at most C − 3, so both halves of a split stay below C |
+| `FictitiousLeafConfig::pressureMergedLimit` | component API | C (23 at C23) | 2 to C |
+| `FictitiousLeafConfig::choiceSeed` | component API | 1 | any |
+
+`PagedBTreeConflictSet::configureFictitiousLeaves` applies them to an empty tree and throws `std::invalid_argument`
+outside these ranges. No server knob sets them: the Resolver uses the defaults. Loading a tree state in the measuring
+harness requires both reserves at zero.
+
+Removed in v105: the switch that disabled the reserve, replenishment limited to startup, pair priority by fictitious
+units, and pressure-fusion limits taken from probabilistic fusion (17 merged, 6 per source). Probabilistic leaf fusion
+after overwrites is unchanged.
+
+## Pool memory backing
+
+The B+ pool is one region of 64 KiB pages. With an explicit split, key pages come first and index pages follow; the
+boundary between them is aligned to 2 MiB. Each range is mapped, populated and touched when the set is built, before
+any batch.
+
+| `PAGED_RESOLVER_POOL_BACKING` | key range | index range | split |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `both` (default) | 2 MiB HugeTLB | 2 MiB HugeTLB | explicit or automatic |
+| `index` | 4 KiB | 2 MiB HugeTLB | explicit only |
+| `heap` | 2 MiB HugeTLB | 4 KiB | explicit only |
+| `normal` | 4 KiB | 4 KiB | explicit or automatic |
+
+- **HugeTLB** uses `mmap(MAP_HUGETLB)` from pages reserved in the system (`vm.nr_hugepages`). FDB never reserves them.
+  There is no fallback.
+- **Errors.** A configuration the backing cannot honour (`index` or `heap` with the automatic split, an invalid split,
+  HugeTLB outside Linux) fails with `PagedPoolBackingInvalid` and `invalid_option_value`. Failing to obtain the memory
+  fails with `PagedPoolBackingFailed`, naming the range, the bytes and pages requested and the system's reason, and
+  `platform_error`. Both are traced at severity error when the set is built; a failure after part of the pool was
+  mapped releases the whole reservation.
+- **Normal pages** are mapped without population, marked `MADV_NOHUGEPAGE` and only then touched, so transparent huge
+  pages never back them, whatever the system's THP mode.
+- **Rounding.** A HugeTLB range is mapped in whole 2 MiB pages: the useful bytes and the page split do not change;
+  only the physical memory grows, by less than 2 MiB per range: the key range ends and the index range starts on the 2 MiB boundary between them.
+- **Pages to reserve.** With an explicit split each range is rounded on its own: {180,364} needs 6 + 12 = 18 pages,
+  {314,358} 10 + 12 = 22 and {0,464} 15. With the automatic split the whole pool is one range (544 pages: 17). Reserve
+  at least that per resolver.
+- **Report.** `PagedPoolBacking` traces the backing, whether the split is automatic, and for each range the useful
+  bytes, the mapped bytes and the page size, verified in `/proc/self/smaps` for HugeTLB ranges, plus the setup time.
+  `PagedBTreeConflictSet::poolBacking()` returns the same report; `skiplisttest` prints it as `PoolBacking:`.
+- `MemoryConfig::backing` selects it in the component API; its default there is `normal`.
+- HugeTLB backing requires Linux; elsewhere only `normal` is accepted.
+
+## Inactive knobs
+
+`PAGED_BTREE_CLEANUP_LEAF_BUDGET`, `PAGED_BTREE_CLEANUP_MERGE_CANDIDATES` and
+`PAGED_BTREE_MERGE_PRESSURE_FREE_PAGE_RATIO` are still read and validated when the set is built, but have no effect:
+the B+'s `cleanup()` does nothing. Expired history is removed locally by each write and reclaimed at capacity, and
+node merges no longer exist.
+
+## Kept compile-time parameters
+
+`PAGED_BTREE_NODE_CAPACITY` (default 23), `PAGED_BTREE_INLINE_KEY_BYTES` (64 from v105; 16 before), `PAGED_BTREE_INLINE_ONLY_KEYS` (1),
+`PAGED_BTREE_NODE_PREFIX` (0), `PAGED_BTREE_BITMAP_CLEANUP` (3), `PAGED_BTREE_WRITE_PREFETCH`,
+`PAGED_BTREE_CLEANUP_PREFETCH`, `PAGED_BTREE_PERMUTATION_SIMD` and `PAGED_RESOLVER_SEARCH_STATS`. They change the
+layout of every unit that includes the header and must match across all of them.
+
+## The two configurations
+
+Both with the build's defaults (C23, K64, inline-only) and these server knobs:
+
+```
+--knob_resolver_use_paged_conflict_set=1 --knob_paged_resolver_use_btree=1
+--knob_paged_btree_reader_group_size=3            # default configuration
+--knob_paged_btree_reader_group_size=2            # alternative configuration
+--knob_paged_resolver_pool_backing=both           # default; normal, index or heap otherwise
+```
+
+`skiplisttest`, for example, with an explicit 180/364 split:
+
+```
+fdbserver -r skiplisttest -C fdb.cluster -s 1 --knob_resolver_use_paged_conflict_set=1 \
+  --knob_paged_resolver_use_btree=1 --knob_paged_resolver_pool_pages=544 \
+  --knob_paged_btree_key_pages=180 --knob_paged_btree_index_pages=364 \
+  --knob_paged_btree_reader_group_size=3 --knob_paged_resolver_pool_backing=both
+```
