+# BTreeTests — the paged B+ conflict set's battery
+
+`BTreeTests.cpp` tests `paged_btree::PagedBTreeConflictSet`
+([PagedBTreeConflictSet.h](../../PagedBTreeConflictSet.h),
+[.cpp](../../PagedBTreeConflictSet.cpp)): one index of disjoint half-open intervals, each carrying
+the CV of its latest write. Its nodes hold fifteen slots in key order through a nibble permutation,
+split proactively at critical nodes and merge under a margin for critical children. It shares the
+existing `PagePool` and `TemporalHeap` with the paged skip list.
+
+Every result here comes from the working tree of 2026-09-29, on top of `181b08fd41`, with
+uncommitted changes: the B+ sources, their integration and this battery. No commit identifies it.
+
+## Provenance
+
+The component and the first eleven cases came as a prototype patch, tested by its author with
+GCC 13 against local stand-ins for FDB's types and knobs (`tests/stubs/`, not supplied). The
+patch reported 11/11 in a normal build and 11/11 under ASan/UBSan. Those results are the
+patch's own, recorded in `../results-normal.txt` and `../results-asan-ubsan.txt`. They were not
+reproduced here: without the stubs they cannot be, and they are superseded by the runs below
+against the real FDB libraries.
+
+Changes to the patch's cases: `main` also catches FDB's `Error`, reports it apart from a failed
+named check, runs every case and exits non-zero if any failed. It used to stop at the first
+`std::exception` and let an `Error` terminate the process. Eleven cases were added.
+
+## Environment
+
+| | |
+|---|---|
+| Image | `foundationdb/build:rockylinux9-latest` |
+| Compiler | clang 19.1.5, `-std=c++20 -stdlib=libc++ -Wall -Wextra -DPAGED_RESOLVER_SEARCH_STATS=1`, `-O2` |
+| Linked against | the build tree's `libfdbserver_core`, `libfdbclient`, `libfdbrpc`, `libflow`; `PagedTemporalSkipList.cpp` is compiled in once, for `PagePool` and `TemporalHeap` |
+| Include flags | those of the real build for `PagedBTreeConflictSet.cpp`, read from `build.ninja` |
+| Sanitizers | `SANITIZE=1`: `-O1 -g -fno-omit-frame-pointer -fsanitize=address,undefined -fno-sanitize-recover=undefined`, run with `ASAN_OPTIONS=detect_leaks=0`. Instrumented: `PagedBTreeConflictSet.cpp`, `PagedTemporalSkipList.cpp` and the battery. Not instrumented: the four FDB libraries and libc++ |
+| Coverage | the same line at `-O0` with `-fprofile-instr-generate -fcoverage-mapping -fcoverage-mcdc`; `llvm-profdata merge -sparse`, `llvm-cov show --show-branches=count --show-mcdc`, `llvm-cov export --summary-only`, LLVM 19.1.5 |
+| Runner | `../run-btree-tests.sh [build-dir]`; `SANITIZE=1`, `BTREE_SRC=<dir>` (the component from a scratch copy, for mutations), `BTREE_TIMEOUT`, `BTREE_SKIP_LIBS=1` |
+
+The battery is a standalone program with its own `main`. It lives in `btree/`, which
+`fdb_find_sources` does not collect, so it never enters `fdbserver_resolver`. It reaches private
+members by including the header with `private` defined as `public`, in its own translation unit
+only. A failing `ASSERT` inside the component throws `Error`, reported as `FAIL by FDB error` with
+its code; a failing named check reports its expression and line.
+
+## Methodology
+
+**Structural verifier.** `verify()` walks the whole tree after the operations that matter and
+checks:
+
+- the live intervals in key order, disjoint and non-empty;
+- uniform leaf depth;
+- parent and slot of every child;
+- the leaf chain in both directions;
+- `maxCV` equal to the maximum of the entries below, expired included;
+- every live separator equal, in key and CV, to its child's first live interval;
+- the critical-children invariant, `slack >= critical children`, and each node's `critical` flag;
+- the permutation against the occupancy bitmap;
+- every node in exactly one occupancy queue, the one of its size;
+- the free list;
+- every node page's live count, and the page budget.
+
+**Independent model.** The model cases keep the original writes, `[begin, end)@cv`, and answer a
+read by scanning them, without separators, fragments or the tree. Writes at or below the boundary
+are dropped from the model, since no admitted read can conflict with them. A mismatch reports the
+seed, batch, query, read version, boundary, both keys in hex, and the two answers.
+
+**Tripwire.** Two cases replace the pool's reclaimer with one that counts reclamation by use
+(heap or index) and records whether a reclaim run for an index reservation reached the version
+being written. While the index is being modified it counts any reclamation as a violation. To
+make an allocation during the modification observable, one case exhausts the pool with
+`tryAcquire` and hides the free-node list between the reservation and the end of the modification.
+
+## Invariants reviewed against the code
+
+The README states these invariants; each was checked against the code and the tests, not taken
+from it.
+
+| invariant | where the code keeps it | tests | mutations it catches |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Live intervals ordered and disjoint | `overwrite` removes every strictly overlapping live interval, starting at the leaf `findLeaf(begin)` reaches: the only live interval that can contain `begin` lies in the child with the last live separator `<= begin`, because any later separator would begin inside it | `verify`, `fragments`, the three model cases | `touchingWriteOverlaps`, `childRankTakesNextChild`, `rightFragmentLost` |
+| `maxCV` exact over stored entries | `refresh` recomputes from the entries after every structural change, through `refreshUp` | `verify` | `purgeDoesNotRefreshUp` (by the criticality it leaves stale), `pruneOneVersionTooMany` |
+| Fragments keep their CV and reuse endpoint references | `overwrite` copies the old entry and replaces only the cut endpoint with the incoming record's: `left.end = incoming.begin`, `right.begin = incoming.end` | `fragments`, `sharedFragmentEndpoints`, `wideOverwriteAcrossLeaves` (references compared field by field) | `fragmentTakesIncomingCV`, `rightFragmentKeepsOldBegin` |
+| No key read after its page is reclaimed | an endpoint's source CV is at least its interval's CV, so a reclaimed page implies an expired interval; every read of a leaf key is preceded by a CV check against the boundary or the read version. A separator carries the CV of the entry it came from and is refreshed before its key is read once that CV expires, including when its child still has live entries | `separatorLifetime`, `separatorExpiryAtAllLevels` (sources of CV 1 at every internal depth), `sharedFragmentEndpoints` (pages retired one by one) | `separatorNeverRefreshed`, `overwriteReadsExpiredEntries`, `liveRanksIncludeExpired`, `reclaimDoesNotAdvanceBoundary` |
+| Reservations complete before paths are computed or the tree is modified; no pool allocation during the modification | `addRange` appends the record and reserves six nodes, re-checks the boundary, and only then calls `overwrite`, which takes nodes only from the reservation (`take` asserts it is not empty) | `noAllocationDuringModification` (12,000 writes under the tripwire, none allocated) | `reserveTwoNodes`; `takeAllocatesWhenEmpty` survives, see below |
+| A write is discarded if reclamation reaches its CV, and its reservation returned | the check after `reserve`, then `releaseReservation` | `ownVersionReclaimed`, `reclamationDuringReservation` (a deterministic reclaim inside the reservation reaches the version; randomized runs with discards followed by admitted batches) | `discardKeepsReservation`, `noDiscardCheckAfterReserve` |
+| Splits and merges keep room for critical children | a split cuts the deepest critical node on the path; each half of a critical internal node weighs at most 10 of 15, so a child that becomes critical cannot cascade past it. `tryMerge` refuses a result whose entries plus critical children exceed 15, and a merge removes one entry from the parent while adding at most one critical child | `verify` everywhere; `criticalMarginOnMerge` (siblings of 5+5 entries: all ten leaves critical, merge refused; four critical, merge made) | `mergeIgnoresCriticalMargin`, `splitHighestCriticalNode`, `noProactiveSplit`, `criticalLeafAtFourteen` |
+| At most one split per inserted entry; an overwrite inserts up to three | one `split` call per `insertInterval`; `overwrite` calls it for the left fragment, the write and the right fragment | `proactiveSplits`, `mixedModel` (per batch), `splitBoundAfterMerges` (per write, after 600 merge passes with internal merges) | `splitHighestCriticalNode` |
+
+Two findings of the review that change no behavior:
+
+- **A write takes at most four nodes, and six are reserved.** At most one root split can happen
+  per write: a fresh root holds two entries and cannot be critical. So three insertions take at
+  most three siblings and one root. Across 12,000 writes the most any write took was 3. Hence the
+  two surviving reservation mutations below.
+- **Two checks never fire.** The `maxCV` pruning at the top of `step` never fires, since a child
+  is pushed only when its entry's CV, its `maxCV`, exceeds the read version; the parent does the
+  pruning. And the separator refresh in `step` is redundant, since `prepareSearchNode` refreshes
+  every live separator of a node before it is processed and the boundary does not move within a
+  query batch.
+
+## Results
+
+**22 cases, 22 passed**, in the ordinary build and under ASan and UBSan. The sanitizers reported
+nothing on the final battery. The compiler reports no warning in the component or the battery.
+
+| case | property | origin |
