+## searchBoundaries (2026-10-02, branch hetzner/searchBoundaries)
+
+**The change.** In the sorted sweep, internal nodes at depths 1 and 2 (`SearchBoundaryDepths`)
+locate each child's cut by bisection instead of the linear scan.
+- **Where.** The search runs over `[position, frame.end)`, from the previous cut to the end of the
+  node's span, reusing the separator's address in every probe.
+- **The predicate is the scan's own.** An endpoint not below the separator, `compareSeparator <= 0`,
+  closes the child. Bisection ignores whether queries are resolved, which keeps it monotone; the
+  resolved endpoints that follow are then passed in a separate step, as the scan passed them.
+- **Unchanged.** The child's minimum read version is computed over `[position, cut)` without
+  comparisons. Lower levels, leaves, CV pruning, resolved queries, `endIndex` and open intervals
+  are not touched.
+
+**New case.** `sortedSearchBoundariesShapes`.
+- **Shapes.** Trees of heights 1 to 5 (C15 and C23) with:
+  - points and ranges at every top-two-level separator, just past it, and ending exactly at it;
+  - repeated endpoints;
+  - open ranges across many children, old enough to stay open and new enough to resolve early;
+  - empty, reversed and out-of-range queries;
+  - 2,000 random ones.
+- **Checks.** Decisions against the scalar search (`checkSortedQueries`), and comparisons at depths
+  1 and 2 within the bisection bound.
+
+**Results.** 107/107 in the eight builds (ASan/UBSan clean). With the cut cross-check copy
+(`sb-cross.py`), where every bisection also runs the linear scan and asserts the same cut and the
+same minimum read version, 107/107 in C15 and C23.
+
+**Mutations.**
+
+| mutant | battery | with the cut cross-check |
+|---|---|---|
+| predicate `< 0` (an endpoint equal to the separator goes left) | 107/107: **equivalent for decisions** | 104/107, by the cross-check's assertion |
+| resolved endpoints after the cut not passed | 107/107: **equivalent for decisions** | 104/107, by the assertion |
+| child's minimum read version not computed | 104/107, reported failures | — |
+| bisection stops one short of the span's end | 106/107, a reported failure | — |
+
+Why the first two are equivalent: a begin at the separator that ends up in the left child is
+consumed at that child's end and carried as an open interval into the next. Resolved endpoints
+are ignored wherever they fall. Both change the cut and not the result, so only the cross-check,
+not the battery, detects them.
+
+**Fixtures with prefixNode on.** `sortedSearchBoundariesShapes` and `sortedResolvedSkipCountedOnce` take
+separators as keys. They decode them with `plainSeparator` before `entryBytes`, since with
+`PAGED_BTREE_NODE_PREFIX=1` an encoded separator holds only its suffix. This correction came with the
+prefixSweep patch; it changes no component code.
+
+### The resolved-endpoint count (2026-10-03)
+
+**The defect.** `8a047b0eb0` counted twice the resolved endpoints passed after the bisection: once in
+the loop that passes them, and again in the walk over `[position, cut)` that computes the child's
+minimum read version. Only the counter `sortedResolvedEndpointsSkipped` was wrong. Decisions, cuts
+and pruning were not affected. The loop now only passes them, and the walk counts each one once.
+
+**Why the battery missed it.** No check related that counter to anything exact. Two counters,
+compiled only with the search statistics, now make it checkable:
+- `sortedLeafResolvedSkipped`: the part counted by leaves;
+- `sortedInternalResolvedSkippedByDepth`: the part counted by internal cuts, by depth.
+
+**Checks.**
+- **Identity**, checked after every sorted read in `checkSortedQueries`:
+  `sortedResolvedEndpointsSkipped` equals the leaf part plus the sum over depths. Each depth's part
+  is at most the batch's endpoint count, since one depth's cuts partition the endpoints they walk.
+- **New case `sortedResolvedSkipCountedOnce`.**
+  - 20,000 points, five ranges from the first key to just past the root's third separator, at read
+    version 0.
+  - They resolve in the root's first child, and their five ends are the first endpoints at or after
+    the boundary of the second child's cut.
+  - It checks that the root's part is exactly 5.
+
+**Results.** 108/108 in the eight builds (ASan/UBSan clean).
+
+**Mutations** (C15 and C23, the same results):
+
+| mutant | result |
+|---|---|
+| the double count of `8a047b0eb0`, restored: the passing loop counts the total again | 104/108, reported failures: the identity, in the new case and in three earlier ones (`sortedSearchBoundariesShapes`, `sortedPruneResolvedAndEmptyChildren`, `sortedEndpointModelUnderPressure`) |
+| the loop also counts the root's part | 107/108, a reported failure: the root's part is not 5 |
+
+**Not covered.** The identity checks the counters against one another, not against the linear
+scan. The cut cross-check (`sb-cross.py`) compares cuts and minimum read versions, not counters.
