+## Inline-only by default, and stableHints (2026-10-02)
+
+**Inline-only by default.** From `f1d2df01b6`, `PAGED_BTREE_INLINE_ONLY_KEYS` defaults to 1, and
+"inline-only off" replaces "inline-only" among the eight builds.
+- Every inline-only case now runs in the default build.
+- Three mutants send a 16-byte key to the heap: a range begin, a range end, a point. Each is caught
+  as reported failures (97, 95 and 97 of 99 cases pass).
+
+**stableHints.** Write hints survive a reclamation inside a batch.
+- **`drainRetired()`** returns the retired nodes to the free queue without discarding hints or
+  closing the batch. `finishWriteBatch()` still closes the batch: it clears the hints, drains and
+  ends it.
+- **Inside `reclaim()`,** the nodes it removes go straight to the free queue (`reclaiming_`), as they
+  did when it closed the batch.
+- **A hint names `{node, incarnation}`.** The incarnation lives in `ReclaimLink`'s padding before
+  `maxCV`, and two `static_assert`s keep it there and keep the link at 24 bytes.
+  - It changes in one place only, `addFree`.
+  - `take()` keeps it.
+  - It saturates at `UINT32_MAX`; a saturated node keeps working and accepts no hint.
+- **`resolveWriteHint` rejects an incarnation mismatch before reading anything else.** It counts
+  it in `writeHintStale`, and still checks `allocated`, `leaf`, `maxCV` and the key range.
+- **Reading the incarnation is safe because a B+ pool is always partitioned:** index pages never
+  leave it. The constructor asserts it.
+
+**Sizes.** `Node` is unchanged in all 36 supported configurations (C 15/23/31, K 0/16/32, prefix
+and inline-only on or off): 960 to 3,392 bytes, alignment 64. `ReclaimLink` stays 24 bytes and
+`HistoryPage` 80. `LeafHint` grows from 8 to 16 bytes. The hint vector is sized to the batch: about
+2,360 writes per batch in skiplisttest, so about 19 KiB more per batch. It is not pool memory.
+
+**Cases.**
+- **New:**
+  - `stableHintLeafReusedAsLeaf`: a leaf freed and taken again as a leaf elsewhere;
+  - `stableHintLeafReusedAsInternal`: a leaf taken again as an internal node;
+  - `stableHintExpiredLeafNotReused`: a leaf whose entries expire. Same incarnation, rejected by
+    `maxCV`.
+  - `stableHintSplitMovesBoundary`: the right move after a split;
+  - `stableHintDrainSatisfiesWithoutAdvance`: no CV-list candidate, no boundary move, batch and
+    hints kept;
+  - `stableHintIncarnationSaturates`: the counter set to `UINT32_MAX - 1`. The node comes back as a
+    leaf, receives writes, and its hints are rejected.
+  - `stableHintGroupedMatchesScalarUnderIndexReclamation`: grouped batches under index-only
+    reclamation against scalar insertion. Same contents and boundary after every batch, one
+    search per batch.
+- **Adapted:**
+  - `groupedWritesKeepHintsThroughReclamation` (formerly `...Restart...`): heap reclamation with
+    pending hints, one search;
+  - `inlineOnlyPressureKeepsHints`: one search per batch;
+  - `indexReclamationUsesAgeList`: hints kept in a batch;
+  - `pendingSurvivorSurvivesAllocationReclaim`: its simulated reclamation drains, it no longer
+    closes the batch.
+- **Corrected after review:** `writeHintRejectsMovedMinimum` and `retiredNodesWaitForBatchEnd`.
+  They built `LeafHint{node}` with incarnation 0, so they were rejected by identity instead of the
+  reason they test. They now take the node's incarnation and check that `writeHintStale` does not
+  move.
+
+**Results.** 106/106 in the eight builds, ASan/UBSan clean. The pool and skip-list batteries pass,
+since their shared header changed.
+
+**Mutations,** each in the default build:
+
+| mutant | result | how it is caught |
+|---|---|---|
+| `resolveWriteHint` skips the incarnation | 103/106 | **counter only** (`writeHintStale`) in three cases: the `allocated`, `leaf` and key-range checks still reject the reused node, so no destination and no decision is wrong |
+| `addFree` does not increment | 102/106 | checks on the incarnation value and the counter; no wrong decision |
+| `take()` resets the incarnation | 103/106 | checks on the incarnation value and the counter; no wrong decision |
+| the counter wraps instead of saturating | 105/106 | the saturation case |
+| `reclaim()` retires the nodes it removes | 100/106 | `std::bad_alloc` in five cases (an exception from the component, not a check), and an extra boundary advance in `indexReclamationUsesAgeList` (a check) |
+| `reclaim()` does not drain the retired nodes | 104/106 | the drain case, and a real divergence: the grouped batch advances the boundary differently from the scalar insertion |
+
+**Not covered here.**
+- **A wrong destination when the incarnation alone fails.** It cannot be produced: the key-range
+  check that follows decides membership. The incarnation protects identity, and its mutant shows
+  up only in the counter.
+- **The pool's partition invariant.** It is asserted, not mutated.
