+## splitMaxCV (2026-10-03)
+
+**The change.** Unconditional; there is no macro.
+- **`split` gives each half its own bound.** After refreshing both halves as before, it takes each
+  half's maximum from `entriesMaximum`, a helper used only by `split`:
+  - a leaf: the largest CV of its entries;
+  - an internal node: the largest of its children's bounds (`maxCV`), without walking their
+    subtrees. Those bounds can themselves stay conservative, so an internal half's new bound is
+    exact only relative to its children's.
+- **Both copies are installed** in the node and in the age vector (`PagePool::lowerNodeAgeAfterSplit`).
+  Neither half moves in the age list, and its incarnation is untouched.
+- **A new root** inherits the larger half's bound and position.
+- **`refresh` is unchanged.** It still only raises a node's bound.
+- **A later raise** still moves a node to the tail. `raiseAge` no longer asserts that a raise reaches
+  the tail's CV, since after a lowering that no longer holds.
+- **Reclamation needs no change:** the boundary advances by `max(validationMinRV, candidate CV)`, so a
+  lowered node met late cannot pull it back. Heap pages are never lowered and keep their order.
+- **History.** A first version made `refresh` return the maximum it scans, to avoid the second scan.
+  That alone cost about 26 M more branch misses and 2% of writing at 32 B head, with the bounds left
+  as they were (performance record, section 64.7 on `hetzner/splitMaxCV`). So the scan is repeated
+  in `split`, which is rare, and `refresh` keeps its code.
+
+**What the verifier checks now.** `verify` no longer requires the age list in CV order. Heap pages
+must still appear in CV order. Bounds, both copies and links are checked as before.
+
+**New cases.**
+- `splitMaxCVLowersInPlaceAndRaisesAtTail`: the pool alone.
+  - A lowered bound in both copies, with links and incarnation untouched.
+  - An equal raise leaves the position.
+  - A raise still below the old tail moves to the tail.
+- `splitMaxCVLeafHalvesAndRoot`: the newest entry on the left and on the right.
+- `splitMaxCVInternalHalvesAndRoot`: both cases check:
+  - each half's bound in both copies;
+  - the new root's bound, position and entries;
+  - the order of every other object in the list;
+  - the contents.
+
+**Results** on this branch (`paged-resolver` with HP's heapLookupInline and writeSeparatorCache)
+are in the commit's `Verified:` line.
+
+**Mutations** (C23; on `hetzner/splitMaxCV`, the same component and cases):
+
+| mutant | result |
+|---|---|
+| `split` does not lower | 111/113, reported failures in the two split cases |
+| only the node's copy lowered, not the age vector's | 88/113, reported failures: the three new cases and the verifier's copy check |
+| a lowered node also moved to the tail | 112/113, a reported failure in `splitMaxCVLowersInPlaceAndRaisesAtTail` only |
+| `entriesMaximum` skips the last entry | 98/113, reported failures in the split cases and the verifier |
+
+The third mutant is caught only by the pool's own case. In the tree cases the split node is already
+the newest object, so moving it to the tail changes nothing. Moving it is not a correctness error,
+only more delay before reclamation.
+
+**Not covered.** How often reclamation meets the list out of CV order is not exercised with a known
+count. The record counts it in FDB runs: 50 to 65 times per run, always below the boundary.
+
+## Coverage of the component as of step 2 (2026-10-01)
+
+The coverage figures above are for the first version of the B+, with 22 cases, and were not redone
+after it changed. This measures the component of `a33f71357f` with the battery at 80 cases.
+
+**Method.** The battery is built at `-O0` with
+`-fprofile-instr-generate -fcoverage-mapping -fcoverage-mcdc`, with clang and LLVM 19.1.5 in the
+project's image. It uses the real FDB libraries and the include flags of `build.ninja`, as
+`run-btree-tests.sh` does.
