+## Two-pass block reader, by default (v102 candidate, 2026-10-06)
+
+**The change.** `PAGED_BTREE_BLOCK_READER` now defaults to 1: the two-argument entry reads with the block reader,
+sweep below the two upper levels (`PAGED_BTREE_BLOCK_BINARY` 0), `breadthBoundaryLevels` 2 and heap hints on.
+`-DPAGED_BTREE_BLOCK_READER=0` restores the previous path. `PAGED_BTREE_BITMAP_CLEANUP` keeps v101's default, arm 3.
+The reader processes each group of nodes in two passes:
+- **The first pass** decides the inline comparisons. Where a prefix ties, it marks the boundary as a heap candidate
+  and the endpoint's cut as ambiguous. Each candidate's address is then resolved and receives two L1 hints.
+- **The second pass**, only in nodes with candidates, completes the ambiguous cuts with whole-key comparisons.
+
+In the two upper levels each separator is located among the node's sorted endpoints by binary search. Groups
+alternate between two buffers (A/B, then B/C), and consumption is ordered and skips children that receive no endpoint
+and no open query. There is no continuation per comparison, pending queue, query window or suffix round.
+
+**Cases.**
+- `blockReaderOrderedModel`: decisions against the scalar search, with sweep and bisection, three budgets and both
+  prefetch modes. Its read versions differ between queries.
+- `blockReaderNodeWorkReuse`: one filter pass per node, comparisons independent of the search width, and address reuse.
+- `blockReaderEndpointCuts`: separator cuts in the upper levels against the linear sweep, for levels 0, 1, 2 and
+  `MaxHeight`, with comparisons unchanged below the selected levels and in the leaves.
+- `blockReaderLongSuffixes`: keys longer than the inline bytes plus 160, with no query window, suffix round or pending
+  request, and two hints per resolution with prefetch on.
+- `blockReaderHeapCandidates`: the two candidates of an interval with a common prefix are resolved even when one is not
+  used.
+- `breadthGroupSweepPendingSuffixes` and `breadthGroupSweepPendingQueue` replace `breadthGroupSweepSharedWindows`
+  of the previous section.
+
+Two older expectations follow the selector: `inlineShortReadAvoidsHeap` and `inlineLongDifferentPrefixesAvoidHeap`
+select their search-activity checks with `PAGED_BTREE_BLOCK_READER` as with the breadth engine. Their no-heap checks
+are unchanged. The bitmapCleanup cases of v101 are kept.
+
+**Results.** 131/131 with the defaults (block reader, arm 3), with the defaults at C23, and with
+`-DPAGED_BTREE_BLOCK_READER=0`. The reader was also checked against the previous revision, with mutants and with a
+linear cut oracle, in sections 25 to 31 of the HP performance record.
+
+## v104: one reader, fusion always active (2026-10-08)
+
+**The change.** The improvements integrated in v104 are the only behaviour; their switches and the paths they
+selected are gone:
+- **One reader.** The two-pass block reader is the only read engine. `v2`, the cursor engines (`PAGED_BTREE_PIPELINED_READS`,
+  `directSuffix`, `stagedSuffixPrefetch`), `breadthBatch` with `PAGED_BTREE_GROUP_SWEEP`, the separate sorted sweep
+  and `blockBinary` are removed.
+- **Groups always.** Below the top `breadthBoundaryLevels` the reader always prepares in groups of G boundaries, and
+  G is 3 (local boundary mask) or 2. Group size 0 and every other value are rejected.
+- **Suffix proof, prefetch and fusion always.** The suffix proof and the heap prefetch have no off switch, and
+  probabilistic leaf fusion is active in every tree, with its result and source limits as the only parameters.
+- **Pool backing.** A tree's pool is mapped with normal or 2 MiB HugeTLB pages, chosen by
+  `MemoryConfig::backing`. The battery uses normal pages; [PoolBackingTests.md](PoolBackingTests.md) covers HugeTLB.
+
+**Reference.** Tests that used to compare one engine against another now compare the reader against
+`referenceConflict` over `contents(tree)`: the intervals actually stored in the leaves, read without the reader. A
+test that checks a property of the reader under configuration runs it with G 2 and 3. `main` seeds FDB's
+deterministic random generator before each case, so fusion, and with it every case, is reproducible.
+
+**Cases removed** with the engines they tested, 22: `pipelineGlobalGroupsAndModes`, `pipelineChunkedLongKeys`,
+`pipelineAdmissionAndBudgets`, `pipelineRangesAcrossChildren`, `cursorInlineGroups`, `cursorMixedKeysAndGrowth`,
+`lazyLeafStopsBeforeUnneededEntry`, `directLongHeadNeedsNoHeap`, `directPrefetchLongKeys`,
+`directPrefetchInlineStaysInline`, `directPrefetchExpiredHeap`, `stagedSuffixPivotsAreBounded`,
+`stagedFirstSuffixByteDecides`, `sortedResolvedSkipCountedOnce`, `breadthBatchGroupsAndSuffixWindows`,
+`breadthBatchSuffixAddressReuse`, `breadthBatchInlineAndAdmission`, `breadthResumeLengths`,
+`breadthGroupSweepPendingSuffixes`, `breadthGroupSweepPendingQueue`, `breadthGroupSweepNoUnusedHeapResolution` and
+`breadthGroupSweepMonotoneCuts`. The no-heap properties they shared are kept by `inlineShortReadAvoidsHeap`,
+`inlineLongDifferentPrefixesAvoidHeap` and `inlineOnlyNoHeapAcrossLevels`.
+
+**Cases kept and retargeted to the single reader:**
+- `pointSuffixEmbeddedZeros`, `suffixVariableLengths` and `suffixFirstByteDecides` were the `direct*` suffix cases;
+  their decision checks now run with G 2 and 3, the first with at most two heap resolutions for one stored point.
+- `readerConfigValidation` replaces `pipelineConfigValidation`. It rejects G 0, 1 and 4, a line size that is not a
+  power of two or exceeds 4,096, `breadthBoundaryLevels` above `MaxHeight` and an L2 reserve that leaves no budget,
+  each before any query flag changes. It also checks `configureReader`.
+- `checkSortedQueries` now runs the reader with G 2 and 3 and levels 0, 2 and 3, and keeps the checks on endpoint
+  order, `endIndex` links, query identity and empty open lists. The counters of the removed sorted engine
+  (`sortedResolved*`, `sortedLeafEntriesSkippedByRV`) are gone. `sortedEndpointBoundariesAndCrossings` requires a
+  skipped node or child, and `sortedPruneResolvedAndEmptyChildren` no longer requires empty children: with many
+  overlapping open queries the block reader has none to skip. `sortedReusePointEndpointAddress` allows two heap loads
+  (begin and end are resolved separately) and prints them.
+- The five `blockReader*` cases run with G 2 and 3 instead of the removed search and prefetch modes.
+
+**Cases rewritten for fusion.** With fusion active a leaf that shrinks may join a neighbour, so:
+- `mixedModel`, `inlineSharedPrefixModel` and `splitBoundAfterMerges` require no internal merge, instead of no merge.
+- `criticalMarginOnMerge` accepts a node that lost one child to fusion and fills the children it actually has.
+- `bitmapAnchorSweepBeforeComparisons` checks the entries that came from the anchor—exactly its live points and the
+  new point, in order, all live—wherever fusion puts them. It bounds a neighbour's contribution by its size, since
+  local cleanup may also drop an expired neighbour entry, and checks the range write against the stored intervals.
+  The scenario runs under 16 seeds and requires both outcomes, with and without fusion.
+
+**New case.** `probFusionLimitsAndSameParent`, on real leaves under one parent:
+- A seven-entry leaf with room beside it is never examined, over 64 seeds.
+- A six-entry leaf does not fuse into a neighbour of exactly `maxMergedEntries − maxSourceEntries + 1` entries,
+  whose sum fits the node but exceeds the result limit.
+- A leaf whose neighbour lies under another parent does not fuse.
+- An allowed fusion keeps every entry in order, removes one child from the parent and leaves internal nodes alone.
+
+**Results.** 109/109 in the seven builds: default (C23, K16, inline-only), ASan/UBSan, no counters, C15, K32/C31,
+K0 and inline-only off. Two runs of the default build give identical output.
+
+**Mutation checks** (scratch copies, full battery):
+
+| mutant | result |
